<title>UK: Britain sees compensation for French trucks strike.</title>
<headline>Britain sees compensation for French trucks strike.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-11-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Prime Minister John Major expressed concern on Tuesday about the impact of the French truck drivers' strike on British firms and said he would expect the Paris government to pay compensation.</p>
<p>&quot;We have indicated to the French government that we would expect claims for compensation for loss of earnings or damage to perishable stock to be met,&quot; Major told parliament at question time on Tuesday.</p>
<p>He said Transport Secretary Sir George Young had already written to his French counterpart urging an end to the strike and underlining the compensation issue. &quot;I won't hesitate to raise the matter elsewhere if necessary,&quot; Major said.</p>
<p>During the day the drivers tightened their stranglehold on French highways and fuel supplies for the ninth successive day to press their wage demands.</p>
<p>-- London newsroom +44 171 542 7767</p>
